This adds an API for dynamic arrays to the C library. Currently that's only for C-level arrays but we could add similar APIs for integers, doubles, etc. Example usage:

```c
struct snapshot_data {
  r_ssize retained_size;
  r_ssize retained_count;
};

struct r_dyn_array* p_data_arr = r_new_dyn_array(DATA_INIT_SIZE, sizeof(struct snapshot_data));
KEEP(p_data_arr->shelter);

// Push a zero-initialised element
r_arr_push_back(p_data_arr, 0);

// Push actual data
struct snapshot_data data = { .retained_size = 1, .retained_count = 2 };
r_arr_push_back(p_data_arr, &data)
```

- Genericity is achieved by taking void pointers and sizes.
- The growable array is allocated in a separate raw vector. This way it can be grown independently and pointers to `struct r_dyn_array` stay valid at all times.
- Both raw vectors are stored in a list shelter. Only this list needs to be protected by callers.

